<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Why I Write
</h4>

<p>
The desire to make sense of things, to create an ordered experience of the world, which is really the root motive behind iii -- otherwise we' all just become historians. This desire unites all writers, I believe, from poets to mere journalistic scribblers. J-profs, managing editors and such types tell us we're not writing for ourselves. But really we are, if our work is any damn good. Ask yourself while you wrestle with a piece: What am I doing, here? Why this struggle? Because making order of the evidence, of your thoughts, of the language is never easy. <br />
While we're thinking about all this, I have another question that has perplexed me: Think of the full range of journalism  and of the full range of fiction. Are their human "truths" that fiction writers can get at that are simply beyond journalism? Is the fiction writer in a better position than us non-fiction types to deal with certain subjects? What, aside from learning to handle the particularities of time and place and the techniques of pacing and suspense, do fiction writers still have to teach us?
</p>
