<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Why I Write
</h4>

<p>
I'd forgotten the part where Orwell touched on something as simple as a fascination for set type. Having changed the font and margins on a big piece of work more times that I'd like to admit in the last month or two, just to keep my brain going, I really do think there's something deeply instinctive, going back to the Rosetta Stone at least, about just putting some words on a surface, and admiring them. <br />
<br />
I would say the only barrier left to my full enjoyment and participation in the Web future of journalism is this loss of the tactile. <br />
<br />
I'm a neat freak about words and type and layout. As a result, I've always marveled at colleague writers who seemed completely unburdened by the physical form of the words, never noticed or cared when the paper changed fonts, or didn't know, back when, how to code a dingbat, or how to H&amp;J their stories at a wider margin. (Or undo the hyphenate-and-justify, with my favorite all-time key on the "old" system: "unjust &amp; clean." Henry Allen, my editor, said a long time ago that someone should start a rock band called Unjust &amp; Clean.)<br />
<br />
Anyhow, the question: why I write?<br />
<br />
I like to make myself laugh. (How awful is that?)<br />
<br />
Joan Didion gave a lecture called "Why I Write" (inspired by Orwell's essay) at UC Berkeley in 1975 and it's been reprinted here and there (never in any of her books, but books _about_ her). While she's perhaps over-emulated and over-quoted, once again she got right to it: (all typos mine, btw)<br />
<br />
"Of course I stole the title for this talk, from George Orwell. One reason I stole it was that I like the sound of the words: Why I Write. There you have three short unambiguous words that share a sound, and the sound they share is this:<br />
I.<br />
I.<br />
I.<br />
<br />
"In many ways, writing is the act of saying _I,_ of imposing oneself upon other people, of saying _listen to me, see it my way, change your mind._ <br />
<br />
"It's an aggressive, even hostile act. You can disguise its aggressiveness all you want with veils of subordinate clauses and qualifiers and tentative subjunctives, with ellipses and evasions -- with the whole manner of imitating rather than claiming, of alluding rather than stating -- but there's no getting around the fact that setting words on paper is the tactic of a secret bully, an invasion, an imposition of the writer's sensibility on the readers most private space." ...<br />
<br />
The whole speech is pretty good, though mostly about her novel writing. (It's also where she said "Grammar is a piano I play by ear.") She's also said many times that the main reason she writes is so she can know what she thinks about something. She doesn't really know what she thinks until the words are on paper or on screen. I'm not sure I totally believe that, but I do understand that part -- the way writing something down provides the very insight, the why, the hunh, the hmmm. I put words on the screen to get to some halfway point of knowing what to think about a subject. (And the words won't come until I've spent time with the subject -- lots of time, or, if not, then I'll spend a few minutes longer than as much time as I've got.)
</p>
