<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Why I Write
</h4>

<p>
I write because I can. It's the most comfortable and natural thing I do in any of my life's roles. It always has been. <br />
<br />
I write because in 1959, when we moved and I started to a new school, I was the only kid in the whole third grade with French braids, freckles and saddle oxfords, and when the teacher announced to the class my story was better than anybody else's, it magically made me smarter and the envy of every pretty girl with loose hair and penny loafers.<br />
<br />
I write because we didn't have a television, cables didn't stretch into West Texas, and every morning at the breakfast table, my dad read the paper, sharing a few of the more interesting stories. Every afternoon after school, I came home and read the paper, front to back, including sports. When I grew up and had the chance, I went to work for the paper my daddy read so faithfully, and I got to write the kind of stories he loved to read at the breakfast table.<br />
<br />
My father's dead and I don't work for the same paper any more.<br />
<br />
I wrote because I could. I did it well enough to get a scholarship to college. Truth be told, going to college might not have been possible otherwise. But I wrote because it organized and gave purpose to my life. People forgive a lot of the shortcomings of the person who's not afraid to lend out her heart and soul to put words on a paper. A bad marriage, leaving home, life in a city, coming back home to West Texas, the gain of a tiny fortune and the loss of it all helped me understand others, and write more knowingly their stories.  <br />
<br />
I don't write as well, and am probably not in the league with the majority of the gangrey group, but I write because I can, and because I am. And there are a million stories out there. I'll get to write a few, and read a few more as long — especially — as you guys are here.<br />
<br />
I write because almost 50 years later I'm still the freckle-faced kid with French braids and saddle oxfords in a loose hair penny loafer world.But when my stories are right, and sometimes they're close enough, I'm back in the league with them, and sometimes, a tiny smidge better.
</p>
