<p>
Thank you all for the great responses. It's 9:30 p.m. on Sunday. My essay is due tomorrow, and I'm just setting down now to write. Your opinions and insights will make this much easier and more interesting. <br />
<br />
I had a thought last night while perusing your answers. What has changed about writing and writers from Orwell's time till now? A lot of people who responded to my initial question said journalism was a great way to make a living, and therein lies an interesting change. In Orwell's time, I don't think journalism was ever thought of as a career, rather it was a calling, an art. With the advent of journalism schools and journalism degrees, newspapering has changed from a calling to a career. I'm not sure what that means for us--perhaps quality suffers as a result because the goal is a paycheck, not something more virtuous like social change. <br />
<br />
Anyway, you've all been helpful and I appreciate it very much. <br />
<br />
-Adam
</p>
